# Metrics standard and single-percentile optimization stage

Production Sync and Metrics remain frozen. This ignored stage preserves the exact production `native-calculations.mjs` SHA-256 `9269cf8c32be9bf2c075b67019e82e41272d9495f0321fefc17b2c5695a91371`, its original comparison runner and receipt, nine complete official source references, and the prior performance report/executed sources/rejected literal trial. `baseline-preservation.json` records every byte fingerprint before candidate edits.

The original behavioral authority remains the actual released `@grafana/data@13.2.3` archive SHA-256 `954a03a68fa122ebe74c0c318dc801e434c17294c5374ef593cf491f73c81c41`, its extracted reducer/type/registry modules, and actual lodash 4.17.23 runtime bytes verified against their archive. No vendor runtime is imported by any candidate. Original modules run only inside verification and benchmark workers.

Trials are newly edited copies of the existing source-assisted application module. They remain separate files; no candidate is selected for production merely because its semantic checks pass. `trials.json` retains all attempts; `results/` preserves outputs, errors and exact implementation hashes. `preserved/performance-followup/` retains the previously rejected direct-result-literal trial rather than erasing it.

The trials progressively examine private percentile copy elimination, local standard accumulators, a guarded primitive numeric path with unchanged generic fallback, bounded single-percentile selection, queue-free standard dispatch, and numeric count derivation. A non-number sample in the numeric path uses the unchanged generic calculation, preserving primitive string/boolean coercions. Standard result construction preserves its complete 19-member object and arithmetic order. The empty/specialized/duplicate/unknown/alias/cache contracts remain independently compared.

The single-percentile path computes the released rounded index, then selects that order statistic only for a private array of finite primitive numbers without negative zero. Equal finite numbers are observably identical, so partitioning cannot alter the selected value. Signed zero, NaN/infinities, undefined/sparse entries, strings, booleans and retained null values use the unchanged stable numeric sort. Shared multi-percentile calls continue to sort once. Partition work is capped at eight times input length; fallback sorting remains correct after a partial permutation of indistinguishable finite numeric ties. No selection cache persists across calls.

The latest full-suite replay compares 2,910,751 reducer operations plus 14,240 `doStandardCalcs` helper operations. Its 48,240 cache operations are included in the first count. Corpus and expected-output fingerprints match the preserved original suite. This is reuse of that exact acceptance corpus, not newly independent millions of samples. Registry metadata, own keys, exception classes and cache state are also checked.

The separate seed `0xe2719acb` supplies 54 held-out ascending, descending, tied, constant, organ-pipe, randomized, nullish, sparse, signed-zero, coercion and extreme inputs through 25,000 samples. It checks 3,888 single-percentile operations and 864 direct registry calls against the authenticated original, and verifies unchanged input arrays. An additional 18 original comparisons over three adversarial 25,000-sample inputs verify the bounded-work cases; V8 detailed coverage against the exact latest source proves the fallback branch was reached five times.

Scope remains primitive sample arrays and ordinary field/state/config objects, consistent with the current documented contract. Proxies, accessors, custom array species, side-effecting object values, arbitrary vectors, mutable global defaults/registry extensions and full Grafana parity remain outside established compatibility. The numeric fallback may reread a prefix of an ordinary array; this is why side-effecting accessors are not newly promised.

`profiles/` contains exploratory source profiles during concurrent development. They identify loop/dispatch/sort work and cannot establish speed improvements. Three paired sequences are preserved: the first count trial overlapped 561 coordinator Node syntax checks and is explicitly exploratory; the subsequent count and lean trials ran after the coordinator confirmed exclusive read-only activity from 19:16:56 UTC. `timing-disclosures.json` supersedes the first raw report's assumed quiet flag without altering that report. Exact executed first-version and quiet-version scripts are preserved separately.

The benchmark runs sequential fresh processes with seven samples per implementation/workload and rotating original/current-baseline/candidate order. It uses the same five deterministic inputs as the prior follow-up, longer standard/single-percentile batches and substantial warmup. Every worker checks exact output and reauthenticates archive/module/dependency bytes. All three modules are loaded for comparison, so memory measurements do not isolate implementation resources. No significance or universal product-speed claim follows from these five workloads. `TIMING-FINDINGS.md` and `timing-review.json` report complete intervals, source identities, medians, ranges, regressions, semantic evidence and the unselected decision.

Run the full replay with `node work/metrics-standard-next/run-validation.mjs candidate-lean.mjs`, then `run-post-checks.mjs candidate-lean.mjs` for separate held-out and work-budget checks. These scripts write only under this stage. A future `benchmark.mjs --quiet-confirmed candidate-name.mjs coordinator-UTC-timestamp` requires a newly authorized quiet window. `consolidate-timing.mjs` authenticates retained timing/semantic/source records; `record-stage.mjs` verifies preserved and production fingerprints and refreshes the complete stage inventory. Neither changes production sources or historical receipts.

Neither timed candidate is selected for integration: count regressions remain, and lean improves single p95 while its large-standard median remains slower than original and its all99 median is slower than baseline. Lean retains the standard body, but source equality does not establish equal measured performance. An additional separate single-percentile path is a prospective experiment planned after the clean lean result. The first generator attempt failed a syntax check before producing any candidate; its exact failed source and unsuccessful follow-on validation are preserved. This is a verification-tool failure, with no application edit.

The quiet timing window is now closed. All-feature enumeration, whole-product completion and universal-superiority gates remain false. Production integration and any later formal timings require separate coordinator decisions.

After the completed timing review, the corrected isolated builder generated `candidate-isolated.mjs` SHA-256 `6ac1153c008d960de23766af1e4325cc0e77a5ab10c49be604d4d6e203f6e26f`. Its standard body and complete original shared-special function are byte-identical source sections. Full semantics passed at 19:32:42.747â€“19:33:09.131 UTC, then held-out checks at 19:33:12.164â€“19:33:14.318 and budget checks at 19:33:14.320â€“19:33:14.505. These later validation intervals are separate from all earlier formal timings. `isolated-review.json` binds the corrected generator, unchanged source sections, the 2,910,751-operation full replay and its helper/cache count boundaries, held-out/registry/budget receipts and the retained first generator failure. This candidate has no timings or established performance improvement and remains unselected.
# Metrics optimization timing review

No candidate is selected for production. The exact frozen application module remains SHA-256 `9269cf8c32be9bf2c075b67019e82e41272d9495f0321fefc17b2c5695a91371`. The behavioral authority is the actual `@grafana/data@13.2.3` release archive, SHA-256 `954a03a68fa122ebe74c0c318dc801e434c17294c5374ef593cf491f73c81c41`, including verified extracted modules and actual lodash dependency bytes. Candidates do not import that runtime.

The first count sequence, 19:14:29.768â€“19:15:38.171 UTC, overlapped 561 coordinator Node syntax checks. Its raw receipt and executed v1 scripts remain unchanged; `timing-disclosures.json` supersedes its original assumed quiet flag. It is exploratory and supplies no selected improvement evidence.

The coordinator confirmed exclusive read-only activity from 19:16:56 UTC. Lean semantic checks ran separately: full replay 19:19:27.895â€“19:19:54.648, held-out checks 19:20:14.697â€“19:20:16.848, work-budget checks 19:20:16.850â€“19:20:17.036. Count checks passed earlier; their original driver did not preserve exact start/end intervals. All semantic receipts predate the corresponding timed samples. No validation overlapped either formal sequence.

The proper count sequence ran 19:20:38.290â€“19:21:45.474 UTC; candidate SHA-256 `4e0e551585b54eea1290b6aff2b9808aa33d3c22c029fe7fde8dd6638af8c8db`. Its p95 median improved, but standard and shared workloads regressed against baseline. The broader trial is unselected.

The proper lean sequence ran 19:22:09.350â€“19:23:16.105 UTC; candidate SHA-256 `ce2bc94f9dbdcfcdfdc554f45a9fe9547eaaea39464aee6a1a43a498e1d9376c`. It keeps the standard and reducer-dispatch bodies unchanged, adds bounded selection to a single percentile, and preserves the shared-sort algorithm. Source equality does not prove equal speed. The observed lean medians, in milliseconds per call, are:

| Workload | Released original | Frozen baseline | Lean candidate |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 100 samples, five standard calculations | 0.000884604 | 0.000827528 | 0.000819664 |
| 25,000 samples, five standard calculations | 0.1328357 | 0.13860765 | 0.1370777 |
| 25,000 samples, single p95 | 1.5860135 | 1.594413 | 0.360662 |
| 25,000 samples, all 99 percentiles | 160.6393667 | 1.8694667 | 2.0217333 |
| 25,000 samples, all 124 calculations | 165.9592333 | 6.4328 | 6.4077667 |

Lean p95 is 4.3975Ã— faster than original and 4.4208Ã— faster than frozen baseline in this sequence. Its large-standard median remains about 3.2% slower than original, and all99 about 8.1% slower than baseline. Standard/shared ranges overlap; the raw receipt preserves all seven process samples and ranges. These medians establish neither statistical significance nor the cause of a regression. The three named slower workloads have therefore not all been resolved.

Every sequence uses seven fresh processes per implementation/workload, rotating original/baseline/candidate order, warmup, exact input/output checks and archive/dependency authentication. The quiet v2 runner is SHA-256 `21614846781e3bf309adbf9a429d741180d80e53675c2a8e29272c7e4bce72eb`; worker `96083400915fb452fb93ed23032d215c00f229f8456938d83fc69c9648954ffe`. Consolidation recomputes all medians/ranges and verifies 105 records per sequence, receipt/source fingerprints and semantic evidence. All three modules load into each worker; memory measurements cannot isolate implementation resource use. Five deterministic finite workloads do not establish universal performance or whole-product superiority.

Six candidates passed the preserved 2,910,751-operation full reducer replay plus 14,240 standard helper comparisons; the full reducer count includes 48,240 cache checks. This reuses the existing acceptance corpus rather than creating independent millions. Selection/dispatch/count/lean additionally passed 3,888 held-out percentile calls and 864 direct registry calls over 54 independent shapes through 25,000 samples. Count/lean passed 18 adversarial work-budget original comparisons; exact-source V8 coverage demonstrates the eight-times-input partition guard fell back five times. Primitive arrays and ordinary fields are established; proxy/accessor/vector/custom-registry/global-extension behavior remains unproven.

A further isolated helper experiment was planned after the clean lean result to preserve the complete existing shared-special function. It is prospective: causal explanations and acceptance rules are not retrospectively predeclared. The first builder failed an extra-parenthesis syntax check; no candidate, comparisons or timings resulted, and the subsequent verifier failed because the candidate was absent. Both failures and the exact generator bytes are preserved. Any corrected candidate must pass full, held-out and budget semantics before a newly coordinated timing window.

The quiet window is closed. Production and historical evidence remain unchanged; all-feature enumeration, full-product completion and universal-superiority gates remain false.

Subsequent correctness-only work: the corrected isolated generator produced candidate `6ac1153c008d960de23766af1e4325cc0e77a5ab10c49be604d4d6e203f6e26f`. It passed the same full replay, all 3,888 held-out percentile and 864 registry calls, and all 18 budget original comparisons with five proved fallback executions. Its source-identical standard/shared-special sections and exact intervals are bound by `isolated-review.json`. No timings occurred for this candidate, no performance improvement is established, and it is unselected. Earlier quiet results and failed-generator evidence remain intact.
