# Proposed local scope additions: Notes, Backup and Vault

This is a work-only proposal for a later crosswalk update. It does not change the requirement registries, their 159 original source/API identities, or their 232 capability IDs. Existing parity and superiority gates remain false. The registries contain 48+42+69 source identities and 68+69+95 capability rows; these counts are not coverage percentages or complete documented-product inventories.

`source-binding.json` authenticates the three registry files, selected current app sources/docs, and the receipts cited below. Receipt results remain attributed to the exact sources used by each run. Later source hashes are recorded separately; historical evidence is not rebound. No tests, original applications, browsers or measurements run for this proposal. Root's current global collection remains separate and pending when this proposal is prepared.

## Notes: Joplin notebook organization

For existing `joplin.notebooks`, replace the narrow description of imported hierarchy plus a flat text field with the bounded implemented workflow: create, rename and reparent a regular local notebook; move a saved note by stable notebook ID; show ancestor paths; and filter by exact ID. Renaming or moving a parent updates descendant display paths while retaining folder/note identities and content. Original user timestamps remain while system update timestamps record moves. Optimistic notebook and note revisions refuse stale writes; organization controls refuse an unsaved browser draft. Shared/trashed notebooks and the reserved conflict notebook are refused by this subset.

Keep the status partial. Notebook deletion/trash, sharing permissions, synchronization, original drag/drop and all client/platform variants remain open. Existing search is AND substring matching, not original full-text search or query grammar. Raw-record retention alone does not implement to-do, geolocation or other original workflows.

The notebook study binds separately to released Joplin Desktop 3.6.14, tag `v3.6.14`, commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`. The official Folder/Note helper slices execute with a fixed clock and in-memory load/save adapter. There are 26 exact candidate comparisons: 20 nesting decisions, four ancestor paths and two note-move effects. Two additional original move executions assert original behavior only. There are zero complete original application, database or GUI executions. Retained original sources carry AGPL-3.0-or-later notices; candidate organization is source-assisted newly written code, without a clean-room or MIT claim.

The production integration receipt records 28/28 checks, zero skips, and source stability, with two separately collected held-persistence cases. Those cases add commit/rollback and dirty-draft evidence; they are not additional original helper comparisons. The old 22-check JEX serialization receipt still binds its earlier source head `c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5`; it is not credited to Desktop 3.6.14.

Bounds remain 8 MiB JEX, 4 MiB per resource, 1,000 notes, 2,000 raw records, 3,000 TAR entries, depth 64, 200-character display paths and the host's 16 MiB atomic record limit. These bounds intentionally differ from unbounded original helpers.

## Backup: restic data pack aggregation and ownership

For existing `RESTIC.REPOSITORY.001` and `RESTIC.CHUNKING.001`, remove the current one-data-blob-per-pack limitation from the local implementation description. Native v1/v2 capture now batches distinct encrypted file-data blobs into sequential data-only packs with a 16 MiB target; tree packs remain separate. Both pending and indexed data IDs deduplicate. Data packs publish before the referencing index and snapshot. Repeat capture preserves prior data pack IDs and adds no rows for already indexed file data.

Keep these capabilities partial, together with `RESTIC.BACKUP.001`, `RESTIC.CHECK.001`, `RESTIC.RESTORE.001` and `RESTIC.LOCKING.001`. The release remains restic 0.19.1, commit `6aa3a516ce654808a1f28f9fa21e9b7c8e6e90bf`. Original CLI check/restore comparisons use synthetic repositories only; the application never launches the original executable. Original source-assisted contracts and BSD notices are preserved.

The production integration receipt records 64/64 selected checks, zero skips, and stable sources. Its private three-file HTTP fixture authenticates header/payload/index structure, restart persistence, data deduplication and restored bytes/mtimes. Two separately identified original CLI commands check/read all data and restore that fixture successfully. Positive original results and deliberate corrupt-input nonzero results are kept distinct. The obsolete `>=7 packs` assertion failed with earlier unbatched Rabin behavior too; its replacement checks authenticated structure/content/dedup invariants rather than enforcing a packing policy. The deterministic in-flight mutation fixture increased from 4 MiB+1 to 24 MiB+1 to cross the aggregate target before EOF; original mutation/check/restore assertions were retained.

An independent exported-helper overlap defect is preserved: a save accepted while a prior flush was held could be dropped. The guarded writer now rejects overlapping save/flush/dispose before input inspection or queue mutation, snapshots accepted caller bytes before awaits, and releases ownership on failure. No prior HTTP data loss was demonstrated because that path awaited each operation. Initial 66-check development, 33-check adapter and 54-check guarded-helper receipts remain separate historical runs, not a combined coverage count.

The earlier bounded Rabin comparisons remain 30 original file cases and 126 ordered chunk entries; batching does not expand their algorithm denominator. Native capture supports regular files/directories and guarded source mutation checks, not atomic filesystem snapshots. Limits remain 64 MiB/file, 512 MiB/snapshot, 96 MiB/pack, 16 MiB metadata/tree, 64 MiB cumulative stored/decoded metadata/tree and a two-minute cooperative deadline. The pack writer accepts at most 8 MiB/blob and 4,096 pending records. Deadline guards cannot preempt blocked filesystem or commit promises. Ciphertext, serialized packs, compression/encryption buffers and cached reads can coexist; neither the target nor the earlier capture-buffer estimate is a process RSS cap.

Original concurrent/randomized batching, remote backends, native retention/repair/prune, full metadata and CLI families remain absent. Failed captures may leave immutable unreferenced packs. No faster runtime, lower memory, greater reliability or security equivalence has been demonstrated for aggregation.

## Vault: KeePass entry history and editor publication

For existing `KP.ENTRY.001`, credit bounded flat history editing and restore rather than mere XML retention: changed supported entry content creates an encrypted previous version; a content no-op updates access without adding a version; imported count/size limits prune supported history using released ordering. Explicit previews remain masked by default. Confirmed restore preserves current identity/location and backs up the current content. Unknown supported XML survives round-trip. Required pruning at the ordinary 9999-12-31 sentinel is deliberately rejected; this is a documented bounded deviation, not original-compatible success.

The separate selected-manual supplement has 199 rows and remains unchanged too. This slice advances parts of its existing `KPM2611-HISTORY-SNAPSHOT`, `KPM2611-HISTORY-LIST`, `KPM2611-HISTORY-COUNT-LIMIT` and `KPM2611-HISTORY-SIZE-LIMIT` contracts. Enforcing imported settings does not implement the original database-settings editor; a local history panel does not establish original entry-dialog behavior.

For `KP.LOCK.001` and the entry lifecycle description, credit guarded publication: dirty editor values refuse Restore before a request; seven-value request signatures plus input revisions retain enabled drafts changed during pending Save/Restore; selection/session generations and captured IDs reject stale publication or wrong-entry mutation. Idempotent view cleanup clears the view's timer/private controls and prevents disposed responses from republishing or rearming it. The independent wrong-entry, same-editor and detached-timer failures remain preserved before their fixes.

Keep all gates partial/open. Release binding is KeePass 2.61.1, authenticated complete release assembly and official source ZIP. The frozen adapter evidence records 41/41 checks, zero skips, 24 original assembly loads and five host child processes. The underlying finite original accounting distinguishes source-composed Save/Touch behavior from actual original `RestoreFromBackup`, `StringToTags`, and `GetSize` calls. Original GUI/Touch workflow executions are zero. A separate independent 11-case regression run and actual private browser/HTTP persistence checks authenticate the accepted final client; these are not additional original assembly comparisons. Root's separately reported current production 41/41 collection and global suite must be cited from their own receipts after root freezes them.

The accepted five runtime identities and exact 82-artifact final independent review were authenticated again by the independent bundle audit. All 1,679 public copy entries exclude `.local`, MZ/PK file prefixes and exact/complete original executable/library/archive payloads. Public stage preservation contains 1,442 artifacts plus two metadata records. Twelve complete source ZIP carriers remain local: four named historical copies plus eight acquisition descendants. Historical assembly prose saying four is preserved unchanged and must not be repeated as the current total. Old 18-check KDBX compatibility, original 181-stage, selection 546-stage and final 1,454-stage evidence retain their own identities.

History is bounded to 1,000 final versions per entry, 8 MiB combined preserved XML/attachment Base64 and flat non-nested histories. A pending 1,001st version can be created and pruned before the final bound. Canonical imported numeric settings are validated; the host's 16 MiB encrypted-record limit can reject atomically. Entry deletion remains permanent in this local UI; recycle-bin behavior, selected-history deletion, full entry-dialog policy, expiration workflows, group management, auto-type and original platform integrations remain open. No security, performance, complete-product parity or superiority claim follows from these finite comparisons.

The current Vault README's older gap paragraph still describes history workflows as absent while its new history section describes this slice. A later documentation update should distinguish this implemented bounded subset from the remaining original history/recycle/dialog policies; the frozen/current-suite source is not edited by this proposal.
